### What is a synchronized method?

A synchronized instance method acquires the monitor lock on `this` when the method begins and releases it when the method returns (normally or by exception).

```java
package com.bank.service;

import java.math.BigDecimal;

public class AccountServiceV1 {
    private BigDecimal balance = new BigDecimal("100000.00");

    public synchronized void debit(BigDecimal amount) {
        // Lock on 'this' acquired here
        validateAmount(amount);              // locked: no shared state accessed
        logTransaction("DEBIT", amount);     // locked: no shared state accessed
        balance = balance.subtract(amount);  // locked: THIS is the only line that needs it
        notifyAudit("DEBIT", amount);        // locked: no shared state accessed
        // Lock released here
    }

    private void validateAmount(BigDecimal amount) { /* validation logic */ }
    private void logTransaction(String type, BigDecimal amount) { /* logging */ }
    private void notifyAudit(String type, BigDecimal amount) { /* audit call */ }
}
```

The problem: `validateAmount`, `logTransaction`, and `notifyAudit` do not access shared state, yet they run while holding the lock. Any other thread calling any synchronized method on the same object is BLOCKED for the entire duration.

### What is a synchronized block?

A synchronized block locks a specific object for only the enclosed critical section. Code before and after the block runs without holding the lock.

```java
package com.bank.service;

import java.math.BigDecimal;

public class AccountServiceV2 {
    private final Object balanceLock = new Object();
    private BigDecimal balance = new BigDecimal("100000.00");

    public void debit(BigDecimal amount) {
        validateAmount(amount);              // NOT locked: runs freely
        logTransaction("DEBIT", amount);     // NOT locked: runs freely

        synchronized (balanceLock) {         // lock ONLY for balance modification
            if (balance.compareTo(amount) < 0) {
                throw new IllegalStateException("Insufficient funds");
            }
            balance = balance.subtract(amount);
        }

        notifyAudit("DEBIT", amount);        // NOT locked: runs freely
    }

    private void validateAmount(BigDecimal amount) { /* validation logic */ }
    private void logTransaction(String type, BigDecimal amount) { /* logging */ }
    private void notifyAudit(String type, BigDecimal amount) { /* audit call */ }
}
```

Only the balance check and subtraction are locked. Validation, logging, and audit notification run in parallel across threads.

### How do the two compare?

| Feature | Synchronized method | Synchronized block |
|---|---|---|
| Lock object | `this` (or `Class` for static) | Any specified object |
| Scope | Entire method body | Only the enclosed code |
| Granularity | Coarse | Fine |
| Multiple locks | No (always `this`) | Yes (different locks for different data) |
| Lock hold time | Longer (includes non-critical code) | Shorter (only critical section) |

### Why is the synchronized block preferred?

Three reasons make the block superior in production code.

First, shorter lock hold time. The block locks only the read-modify-write operation on shared state, not the surrounding validation, logging, or notification. Second, specific lock objects. You can use different locks for different pieces of shared state, allowing unrelated operations to proceed in parallel. Third, private lock objects prevent external code from accidentally synchronizing on `this`.

```java
package com.bank.service;

import java.math.BigDecimal;

public class AccountServiceV3 {
    private final Object balanceLock = new Object();
    private final Object auditLock = new Object();
    private BigDecimal balance = new BigDecimal("100000.00");
    private int auditCount = 0;

    public void debit(BigDecimal amount) {
        synchronized (balanceLock) {
            balance = balance.subtract(amount);
        }
        // auditLock is independent: debit and audit do not block each other
        synchronized (auditLock) {
            auditCount++;
        }
    }
}
```

### What is a static synchronized method?

A static synchronized method locks on the `Class` object (`ClassName.class`), not on any instance. This means all instances share the same lock.

```java
package com.bank.service;

public class GlobalAudit {
    // Locks on GlobalAudit.class
    public static synchronized void runAudit() {
        System.out.println("Running global audit");
    }

    // Equivalent block form
    public static void runAuditExplicit() {
        synchronized (GlobalAudit.class) {
            System.out.println("Running global audit");
        }
    }
}
```

### What are common mistakes?

The most dangerous mistake is synchronizing on an object that can change or that is shared unintentionally.

```java
package com.bank.service;

public class LockMistakes {
    // WRONG: not final, can be reassigned
    private Object lock = new Object();

    public void transfer() {
        synchronized (lock) {
            // Another thread reassigns: lock = new Object();
            // Now threads synchronize on DIFFERENT objects: no mutual exclusion!
        }
    }

    // WRONG: synchronized(this) exposes the lock to external code
    public synchronized void debit() {
        // External code can do: synchronized(accountService) { Thread.sleep(10000); }
        // This blocks ALL synchronized methods on your object for 10 seconds!
    }

    // CORRECT: private final lock
    private final Object safeLock = new Object();

    public void safeDebit() {
        synchronized (safeLock) {
            // Private: no external code can access this lock
            // Final: cannot be reassigned
        }
    }
}
```

### What should a senior engineer know?

- **Lock hold time is the key metric**. A synchronized method that includes a 500ms database call holds the lock for 500ms. A synchronized block around only the 1ms balance update holds the lock for 1ms. The difference is 500x throughput.
- **Bytecode**: `synchronized` methods use the `ACC_SYNCHRONIZED` flag in the method's access flags. Synchronized blocks use explicit `monitorenter` and `monitorexit` instructions.
- **Lock coarsening**: the JIT compiler may merge adjacent synchronized blocks on the same lock into a single block to reduce monitor entry/exit overhead.
- **Lock elision**: if escape analysis proves that an object never escapes the current thread, the JIT can eliminate the synchronization entirely.
